        *   We also need to ensure that the $(k+1)$-th element is *not* $\le x$.
        *   So we need $b_k < b_{k+1}$.
        *   If $b_k < b_{k+1}$, then $x = b_k$ is a valid answer.
        *   If $b_k = b_{k+1}$, then any $x \ge b_k$ will also result in $b_{k+1} \le x$, meaning at least $k+1$ elements are $\le x$. In this case, there is no $x$ that makes exactly $k$ elements $\le x$. So print -1.

